3. Important Java concepts you learned
A. null versus an empty list
These are different states.


java
List<Workout> workouts = workoutSessions.get(101);
workouts == null → no non-null list was returned for that key.

workouts.isEmpty() → a list exists but contains no elements.

The standard safe check is:


java
if (workouts == null || workouts.isEmpty()) {
    continue;
}
Because || uses short-circuit evaluation, Java won't call isEmpty() if workouts is null.

One subtle detail: map.get(key) == null doesn't tell you whether the key is absent or explicitly mapped to null. Use map.containsKey(key) when you need to distinguish those cases.

B. Why use two Maps?
You asked when two Maps are better than one.

Suppose you need both total duration and workout count per membership status:


java
Map<MembershipStatus, Double> total;
Map<MembershipStatus, Integer> count;
One Map tracks total duration; the other tracks workout count.

Use this mental model:

One metric per key → usually one Map.

Two independent metrics per key → two Maps can work well.

Several related metrics per key → consider grouping them in a dedicated value object when your design allows it.

C. How getOrDefault() works

java
total.getOrDefault(status, 0.0)
This returns the existing value or the default if the key is missing.

But it doesn't insert the default into the Map.


java
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.getOrDefault("GOLD", 0); // Returns 0
map.containsKey("GOLD");     // false
If you've already initialized every key, getOrDefault() may be unnecessary. For example, if you know every status has a value, you can use total.get(status).

D. MembershipStatus.values()
Given:


java
enum MembershipStatus {
    BRONZE, SILVER, GOLD
}
Java automatically provides:


java
MembershipStatus.values()
It returns an array of all enum constants.

You used it to initialize every status:


java
for (MembershipStatus status : MembershipStatus.values()) {
    total.put(status, 0.0);
    count.put(status, 0);
}
This ensures even statuses with no workouts appear in your results.

If PLATINUM is added to the enum later, the loop handles it automatically.

E. Sorting by workout ID

java
workouts.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Workout::getId));
This sorts workouts in ascending ID order.

Remember: it modifies the original list. If you need to preserve the stored order, make a copy first:


java
List<Workout> sortedWorkouts = new ArrayList<>(workouts);
sortedWorkouts.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Workout::getId));
F. Traditional switch and break
You learned that traditional Java switch cases can fall through.


java
switch (status) {
    case BRONZE:
        free = 1;
        rate = 10;
        break;
    case SILVER:
        free = 3;
        rate = 8;
        break;
    case GOLD:
        free = 5;
        rate = 6;
        break;
    default:
        throw new IllegalStateException("Unknown status");
}
Without break, execution can continue into subsequent cases and overwrite the values.

Arrow-style switch cases (case BRONZE ->) avoid this fall-through.

